# Build products (regenerated by make). `/build/*` is a blanket exclude
# and every SOURCE file under build/ has to earn its way back in below,
# which is easy to forget when adding one - see the 2026-08-17 note.
#
# build/watcom/{cli,clidos,dll,gui} are OBJECT DIRECTORIES (verified with
# `file`: they are directories of .obj, not scripts - the Watcom recipes
# live in the Makefile). gen_exports.py is the one real source file in
# there, a DLL build-time dependency.
#
# build/arm/*.sh and build/ci.sh are SOURCE: the ARM cross-build, the
# QEMU runner, the x86-vs-ARM bit-identity gate and the CI entry point.
# They were all silently ignored until 2026-08-17 - `/build/*` caught
# them and nothing re-included them - which meant a `git clean` would
# have deleted the gates and a fresh clone would never have seen them.
# Gates that do not survive a clone are not gates. Re-include the
# DIRECTORY before its contents: git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so `!/build/arm/*.sh` alone would not have worked.

/build/*
!/build/watcom/
/build/watcom/*
!/build/watcom/gen_exports.py
!/build/arm/
!/build/ci.sh

# Stray compiler/linker intermediates (gcc, Open Watcom), plus the raw
# dumps probes drop at the repo root when run without a -o path.
*.o
*.obj
*.err
*.out
*.map
*.lib
*.lst
*.mbr
*.sym

# Binaries
/llama98
/llama98.exe
/llama98-w.exe
/llama98-dos.exe
/llama98.dll
/kunkun98-gui
/kunkun98-gui.exe
/kunkun98-w.exe
/kunkun98-serve
/kunkun98-serve.exe

# wrc/windres resource artifacts
gui/kunkun98.res

# Runtime config written beside the binary (kunkun98.ini, kunkun98-gui.ini,
# kunkun98-w.ini) - machine-local, and carries the classic_ui test switch
kunkun98*.ini

# Claude Code / AI assistant local config (machine-specific, not source).
#
# CLAUDE.md is NOT in this list, and the name is why it nearly was: this
# repository's copy is project documentation - iron law zero, the build
# and verify sections, the layering rule - and excluding it meant a fresh
# clone got none of them. The one document whose job is to stop someone
# working in the wrong tree was the one that did not survive a clone,
# which is the same defect /build/* had with the ARM gates. The training
# tree tracks its own CLAUDE.md for the same reason.
#
# CLAUDE.local.md stays excluded: that name is the per-machine one.
.claude/
.claude.json
.claude.json.backup
.mcp.json
CLAUDE.local.md
.aider*
.cursor/
.codeium/
.github/copilot-instructions.local.md

# Scratch / working directories
/tmp/
/.tmp/
/.cache/
/scratch/
/worktrees/
/.worktrees/
/out/
/dist/

# Model weights and test fixtures (never committed - too large)
/models/
*.gguf
*.safetensors
*.pth

# Editor / IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.orig
*.rej

# OS cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Workflow scratch (design notes, plans). This project keeps its
# reasoning at the declaration it belongs to, not in a parallel tree.
docs/superpowers/

# Numeric-law probes (.prof/). Migrated 2026-08-17 from the deprecated
# training-side tree (E:\LLM\llama98-zh\.prof) per user decision - see
# .prof/README.md for the classification and the consequence of this
# ignore rule (not in remote, invisible to a fresh clone). Note the
# tension with tools/kernelgen/gen_t2.py's own header, which objects to
# exactly this pattern for ITS twelve generators ("they are source, this
# is not intentional") - the user decided anyway, deliberately, for this
# directory; that is not the same as the exclusion being unnoticed.
.prof/
